{"candidates":[{"claim":"Idempotent operations satisfy f(f(x))=f(x): repeated application converges to the same state as a single application. Safe operations merely cause no corruption or harm when repeated, but their repeated state may differ from the single-application state. An append-only event log requires only that its writers be safe—retried appends produce duplicate entries but no corruption—while idempotency is deferred to the replay processor, which must deduplicate (via sequence numbers, idempotency keys, or hash-based collapsing) to achieve deterministic replay.","mechanism":"An append to the log is inherently non-idempotent: appending the same event twice yields two entries, a different state from appending once. But it is safe: the log tolerates duplicates without corruption, violating no invariant. Deterministic replay survives because the replay layer applies idempotent processing semantics downstream—it collapses or ignores duplicates. The writer's obligation is merely retry-safety (don't corrupt the log on retry); the system's obligation is idempotent consumption. This separation is why at-least-once delivery is compatible with deterministic replay: safety at the write boundary, idempotency at the replay boundary.","counterconditions":["If the log's consumers cannot distinguish duplicate entries from originals (e.g., no idempotency keys, no sequence numbers, no content hashing), then safe-but-non-idempotent writers are insufficient and the log effectively requires idempotent writes via upstream deduplication","If deterministic replay is defined as byte-for-byte log identity rather than semantic state convergence, then duplicate entries from retries break determinism and the log requires idempotent writers with idempotency keys","If the downstream processor is not itself idempotent (e.g., a counter increment), then safe writers alone do not suffice—every duplicate entry corrupts the replayed state"],"typicality":0.92,"optional_refs":["SRC_005","SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"The published distinction (per RFC 9110 §9.2.1) is sharper than the informal one: 'safe' means the operation has no side effects at all—it is semantically read-only, intended to be callable without any risk of changing observable state. 'Idempotent' means repeated identical invocations have the same effect as a single one, but side effects are permitted. An append-only event log's writers are neither safe nor idempotent in this strict sense. What the log actually requires is a weaker, informal 'retry-safe' property that neither RFC term captures: retried writes may produce duplicate entries, but those duplicates are tolerated by the replay protocol.","mechanism":"In RFC 9110 terminology, safe = read-only (GET), idempotent = convergent (PUT, DELETE). An append is POST-like: it has side effects (new entry) and is not convergent (two appends ≠ one). So strictly, the log's writers are neither safe nor idempotent. The log survives at-least-once because it imposes a third property—call it 'retry-tolerant'—where the replay protocol is explicitly designed to handle duplicates. This property is neither safety (side effects exist) nor idempotency (state diverges), but a protocol-level guarantee that duplicates are benign.","counterconditions":["If the problem uses 'safe' in the retry-safety sense rather than the RFC 9110 read-only sense, this framing collapses and the log does require safe (retry-safe) writers","If the replay protocol has no deduplication mechanism, the distinction is academic—at-least-once delivery would produce incorrect replay regardless of terminology","If one argues that 'no harmful side effects from retry' is itself a form of safety (broadening the RFC definition), then the log does require safe writers under the broader reading"],"typicality":0.75,"optional_refs":["SRC_007","SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"The append-only log actually requires idempotent writers, not merely safe ones, because deterministic replay demands that the replayed system state match the original state. If retried appends produce duplicate log entries that the original execution did not produce, replay is non-deterministic. Only idempotent writes—achieved through idempotency keys that the log layer deduplicates—ensure that the replayed log is semantically identical to the original.","mechanism":"Consider a writer that appends an event without an idempotency key. Under at-least-once delivery, the same event is appended twice. During replay, the processor sees two entries where the original saw one. If the processor is a state reducer (e.g., summing values), the replayed state differs from the original. This violates deterministic replay. The fix is idempotency keys at the write level: the log (or a deduplication layer) collapses duplicate-keyed entries, making the effective write operation idempotent: f(f(x)) = f(x). Safety alone (no corruption) does not prevent semantic divergence.","counterconditions":["If the replay protocol already includes a downstream deduplication step (content hashing, sequence-number collapsing), then idempotency at the write layer is unnecessary—the log only needs safe writers","If deterministic replay is defined as semantic state convergence rather than log-identity, then a downstream idempotent reducer suffices and writers need only be safe","If the events themselves carry monotonic timestamps or sequence numbers that allow the replay processor to deterministically collapse duplicates, then safe writes plus idempotent processing is sufficient"],"typicality":0.55,"optional_refs":["SRC_002","SRC_005"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"The distinction between idempotent and safe is best understood through counterexample. A writer that appends a random UUID per call is safe (retries don't corrupt the log) but not idempotent (each retry produces a different entry). A writer that appends a deterministic hash of the payload is both safe and idempotent (same payload always yields the same entry, deduplicatable). A writer that increments a global counter and appends the result is neither safe nor idempotent (retries corrupt the counter and produce divergent entries). The append-only log requires the safe-and-non-idempotent writer at minimum, but prefers the safe-and-idempotent writer for robust deterministic replay.","mechanism":"Three archetypes: (a) random UUID writer—safe, non-idempotent; the log has duplicates but replay can filter by content hash if payloads are identical. (b) deterministic hash writer—safe and idempotent; the log or processor can collapse exact-duplicate entries. (c) counter-incrementing writer—neither safe nor idempotent; retries corrupt the counter state and produce semantically distinct entries that replay cannot distinguish from genuine new events. The log's minimum requirement is (a): safe writers that don't corrupt external state on retry. The log's ideal requirement is (b): idempotent writers whose duplicates are trivially detectable. The log absolutely cannot tolerate (c).","counterconditions":["If payload content is genuinely unique per logical event (e.g., two distinct payments of the same amount), then a deterministic hash writer would incorrectly collapse them—a case where idempotency keys must be separate from content hashes","If the replay processor has no access to content (encrypted log), then only idempotency keys work, and safe-but-non-idempotent writers require the keys to be present","If external mutable state (like the counter) is also logged as an event, then even (c) can be made replay-safe by treating the counter as a derived projection rather than an independent store"],"typicality":0.6,"optional_refs":["SRC_006"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"The distinction is a category error in the context of append-only logs: idempotency and safety are properties of operations, but the append-only log requires a property of the delivery protocol—namely, that retried writes are distinguishable from original writes by the replay layer. This 'distinguishability' is neither idempotency nor safety; it is a protocol-level invariant that the log enforces through metadata (delivery IDs, producer epochs, sequence numbers). The writers need only attach this metadata; they need not themselves be idempotent or safe.","mechanism":"The real requirement is that the replay layer can tell which entries are duplicates of which. This is a protocol property (delivery metadata), not an operation property (idempotency/safety). A writer that attaches a unique delivery ID to each attempt is neither idempotent (different IDs per attempt) nor formally safe (it has side effects), but the replay layer can collapse entries with matching logical keys. The confusion arises from conflating operation-level properties with protocol-level guarantees.","counterconditions":["If the replay layer ignores delivery metadata and relies solely on content comparison, then distinguishability reduces to content idempotency and this framing collapses","If the log has no mechanism for attaching metadata (raw append of opaque bytes), then the protocol-level invariant cannot be enforced and the log falls back to requiring content-level idempotency","If one defines 'safe' broadly enough to include 'attaches metadata that enables downstream deduplication,' then the log does require safe writers under this broad definition"],"typicality":0.25,"optional_refs":["SRC_008"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}},{"claim":"What the append-only log requires is 'commutative idempotency' of the processing function, not of the writers. The writers need only be safe (retries don't corrupt), while the processing function applied during replay must be both idempotent (f(f(x))=f(x)) and commutative (f(x∘y)=f(y∘x)) so that duplicate entries and reordered entries both converge to the same replayed state. The published distinction between idempotent and safe is thus secondary to the real requirement: the replay function must be a semilattice fold.","mechanism":"An append-only log under at-least-once delivery produces a multiset of events, possibly with duplicates and reorderings. Deterministic replay requires that the processing function F applied to any permutation of this multiset (including multiplicities) yields the same state. This is exactly the definition of a semilattice fold: F is associative, commutative, and idempotent. The writers need only be safe (don't corrupt the log), while F—the replay processor—must satisfy all three algebraic properties. The published idempotent/safe distinction describes writer properties; the actual requirement is on the processor.","counterconditions":["If the log imposes a total order (e.g., sequence numbers), commutativity is unnecessary and only idempotency of the processor suffices","If events are not reorderable (causal dependencies exist), then the semilattice fold is too strong and the requirement reduces to ordered idempotent processing with deduplication","If the processing function is inherently non-commutative (e.g., event sourcing with state transitions), then duplicates cannot be collapsed by commutativity and the log must enforce exactly-once via idempotency keys"],"typicality":0.15,"optional_refs":["SRC_001","SRC_004"],"sidecar":{"search_signal":"productive"}}],"research_proposals":[{"request_identifier":"python-logging-append-handler","purpose":"Check whether Python's logging module documentation discusses append-mode file handlers, retry semantics, or duplicate entry handling relevant to append-only event logs and idempotent replay","urls":["https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.handlers.html"]},{"request_identifier":"python-sqlite3-idempotency","purpose":"Check whether Python's sqlite3 documentation discusses transaction retry, idempotent INSERT semantics, or deduplication mechanisms relevant to at-least-once delivery in append-only systems","urls":["https://docs.python.org/3/library/sqlite3.html"]}]}